==History==
The {{nihongo|'''Matsubara Electric Railway'''|松原電気鉄道|Matsubara Denki Tetsudō}}, commonly known as {{nihongo|'''Matsuden'''|松電}}, is a major [[Okaiken|Sanese]] private railway company serving the northern and southeastern sectors of [[Okami]].

===Founding and Northern Expansion (1912–1952)===
Matsuden was founded in 1912 to connect the northern highlands of the [[Uonuma]] and [[Matsubara]] districts. Unlike the nationalized OR lines, Matsuden focused on developing luxury "garden suburbs" in the north to attract the city's growing middle class. The line terminated at the northern bank of the river and never crossed the Hiryūgawa, funneling commuters into the city center via the early ferry networks before the completion of the [[Miyoshi Line]] of the Okami [[Okami Metro]].

===The Eastern Arc and Post-War Recovery (1952–1980)===
Following the Great Okami Flood, the company shifted its strategic focus toward the eastern plains. In the 1950s, Matsuden began the ambitious construction of its Eastern section. By bypassing the heavy industrial zones of [[Hokuei]], the railway utilized the high ground of the [[Sannō]] and [[Kaminoyama]] districts to create a commuter corridor. This expansion was characterized by intense competition with the [[Keishi]] and [[Hanshi]] railways, leading to the creation of the multi-level hubs at [[Kaminoyama-Meidō]] and [[Kaminoyama-Minami]].

===The Tarumi Extension and Modern Era (1980–Present)===
The final phase of development saw the line turn sharply eastward from the [[Kashiman]] heights to reach the coastal [[Tarumi]] ward. This extension transformed the former fishing villages of Tarumi into a major residential and resort zone. In 1995, the completion of the [[Nagata-Tarumi]] terminal established a vital connection with the '''Subway Nagata Line''', solidifying Matsuden’s role as the primary "Transversal" carrier of the eastern metropolitan area.

